A coin is tossed repeatedly, and the relative frequency of heads is recorded after $10$, $20$, $50$, $100$, $200$ and $500$ tosses.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,6.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.3,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {0/0,1/0.2,2/0.4,3/0.6,4/0.8,5/1} {\draw (-0.12,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.15,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1/10,2.3/20,3.6/50,4.9/100,6.2/200,7.5/500} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {$\lbl$};}
\draw[thick] (1,3.5)--(2.3,2.0)--(3.6,2.8)--(4.9,2.3)--(6.2,2.6)--(7.5,2.5);
\foreach \p in {(1,3.5),(2.3,2.0),(3.6,2.8),(4.9,2.3),(6.2,2.6),(7.5,2.5)} {\fill \p circle (2.2pt);}
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-1.6,2.5) {Relative frequency};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.25,-1.0) {Number of tosses};
\node[font=\small] at (4.25,6.1) {Coin-toss experiment};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. What value does the relative frequency of heads approach as the number of tosses increases?
b. What law does this experiment illustrate?
